---
name: set-distinct
description: "Set is its own value on every lane: dedup, membership equality, union/intersect/diff, shape-preserving map/filter"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `Set` is not a `List`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-tuple-and-set-arms-missing`. Until 2026-08-04 two lanes had no Set at
all. On **v2** the F front lowered `Set(...)` through the *same* `parseListLit` as `List(...)`, so
the two were byte-identical in the IR; on **js** `_setOf(...)` returned a plain array. The entry
recorded this as a display problem. Measured on all four lanes before any code was written, it was
not:

| | `int` / `jvm` (golden / oracle) | `v2` before | `js` before |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Set(1, 1, 2)` | `Set(1, 2)` | `List(1, 1, 2)` — **duplicate kept** | `List(1, 2)` |
| `Set(1,2) == Set(2,1)` | `true` | `false` | `false` |
| `.union` / `.intersect` | `Set(1, 2, 3)` / `Set(2)` | **`Stub`** — the silent-at-exit-0 sentinel | **crash**: `Method not found: union` |

So a Set silently kept duplicates, compared by order, and its two most common operations either
vanished into the `Stub` sentinel or killed the program. This case pins the *value*, not the
rendering — every row below was wrong on at least one lane.

```scalascript
// Construction dedups. The literal is written in ASCENDING order ON PURPOSE: measured 2026-08-04,
// `Set(3, 1, 3, 2)` prints `Set(3, 1, 2)` on `jvm` (real Scala keeps insertion order for Set1..Set4)
// and `Set(1, 2, 3)` on `int` — the golden and the oracle DISAGREE about element order, and this
// case must not silently pick a side. Every set here is written so that insertion order and the
// interpreter's order coincide, which keeps the rows below about the Set itself. The disagreement
// is filed as `int-set-element-order-differs-from-scala`.
println(Set(1, 2, 2, 3))
println(Set(1, 2, 2, 3).size)

// Membership. The `s(x)` apply-form is Scala's membership call and works on the `jvm` lane, but the
// INTERPRETER — the golden — cannot call a Set at all ("Not callable: Set(1, 2)"), so it is not
// pinned here. Filed separately as `int-set-apply-is-not-membership`.
println(Set(1, 2).contains(2))
println(Set(1, 2).contains(9))

// Equality is membership, not order.
println(Set(1, 2) == Set(2, 1))
println(Set(1, 2) == Set(1, 2, 3))

// The operations that used to answer `Stub` / throw.
println(Set(1, 2).union(Set(2, 3)))
println(Set(1, 2).intersect(Set(2, 3)))
println(Set(1, 2, 3).diff(Set(2)))
println(Set(1, 2).subsetOf(Set(1, 2, 3)))
// The INFIX forms reach the arithmetic path, not the method dispatcher, on both non-reference
// lanes — a separate hole from the named methods above, and just as silent: `Set(1, 2) + 3` was
// the string `"1,23"` on js and `Set(1, 2)3` on v2, and `- 1` was `NaN` / `()`.
println(Set(1, 2) + 3)
println(Set(1, 2) - 1)
println(Set(1, 2) ++ Set(3, 4))
// `--`, `&` and `|` are NOT pinned here, and the reason is a different defect: on v2 `&`/`|` reach a
// bitwise primitive that coerces the receiver ("expected Int, got Set(1, 2)") and `--` is a silent
// no-op — measured on LISTS too (`List(1,2,3) -- List(2)` returns the list unchanged where int
// raises), so it is an operator-lowering hole, not a Set one. Filed as
// `v2-set-ops-and-or-coerce-to-int-and-double-minus-is-a-silent-no-op`. js answers all three
// correctly today; a row here would gate js while encoding v2's bug as expected output.

// Shape-preserving: `Set.map` is a Set in Scala, and the result must be deduped AGAIN — `x * x`
// maps two distinct elements onto one. (Multiplication and not `x / 2`, which would have measured
// the unrelated `js-int-division-by-zero`-adjacent gap: `3 / 2` is `1.5` inside a js lambda.)
println(Set(1, 2, 3).filter(x => x > 1))
println(Set(1, 2).map(x => x * 2))
println(Set(-1, 1).map(x => x * x))

// Crossing between the two representations, both directions.
println(List(1, 1, 2).toSet)
println(Set(1, 2).toList)

// Value-returning ops delegate to the list implementations and must NOT be re-wrapped as a Set.
println(Set(1, 2).mkString("-"))
println(Set(1, 2).sum)
println(Set(1, 2).exists(x => x == 2))
println(Set().isEmpty)
```

`Set().isEmpty` is the empty-construction guard: `Set.empty` and `Set()` reach a different lowering
from `Set(a, b)` on both fronts, and an empty Set that came back as an empty *list* would pass every
other row here.
